            <div class="step4-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Personality Traits</h2>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/floral.png">                
                <p>Hero archetypes are selfless in their desire and ability to swoop in and help people.Their fortitude and perseverance are second to none, and challenges to a Hero archetype are like honey to a bee.Strong and brave, the Hero is fearless when he or she stands in the face of their future or of anything that he or she is required to step up and do.</p>
                <p>Heroes are fond of stepping in to assist in situations that have reached their awareness and that they have deemed world-changing enough to need their involvement.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Hero&rsquo;s don&rsquo;t mind telling you all about their efforts either or basking in the glory that comes with their heroism! And while some people might think that this is a little superficial, it&rsquo;s not.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Heroes are proud to contribute, they want to inspire others to do so too.They also need to remind themselves that they are brave because they cannot stand the idea of being a coward.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Enjoying the glory for their efforts is how the Hero attains a sense of contribution.</p>
                <p><strong>Frequently Misunderstood, But Never Afraid To Sacrifice</strong></p>
                <p>To others, the Hero archetype can seem to be cold, pushy and arrogant at times. Others often misunderstand the Hero&rsquo;s need to position themselves at the front of the line to serve the greater good.And because the Hero takes on the responsibility of the challenges put in front of him with every fiber of his being, he or she will sacrifice anything to step up to the challenges they face.It&rsquo;s not unusual for the Hero to sacrifice the respect and patience of others during these times.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>While the Hero archetype wouldn&rsquo;t have things any other way, they do carry the burden of the responsibility of &lsquo;saving&rsquo; and &lsquo;protecting&rsquo; everybody. They also bear the cross that is placed on them by others around them who may or may not receive the Hero&rsquo;s perceived obligations or good (but sometimes misguided) intentions well.</p>
                <p>But when the Hero finds complementary archetypes who take the time to understand them and support them in their need to step up, they find a sense of peace and contentment, which is something that they may have been missing beforehand. This respect and understanding from other people enable the Hero to become a true force to be revered and reckoned with.</p>
                <p><strong>Why Do We Hide Ourselves Even from Ourselves?</strong></p>
                <p>Every person has a hidden side.Jung calls it a shadow aspect, and it&rsquo;s discussed in great detail in our Premium Archetype Reports.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Here at Individuologist.com we realize that there is a hidden side or an unconscious world (to be more precise) that we experience internally, but we don&rsquo;t, or can't show or express this existence outwardly.It&rsquo;s this hidden aspect that can bring about great reward if we were to focus our attention on it. It&rsquo;s also crucial for helping each different archetype overcome their greatest challenge.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Before we get started, it&rsquo;s important to acknowledge that there&rsquo;s also a hidden part of us that remains unconscious even to ourselves and the process of individuation can help us to uncover these hidden aspects of our &lsquo;self&rsquo; too.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>When we start to uncover these &lsquo;hidden&rsquo; aspects of our &lsquo;self,&rsquo; we begin to learn about what is really driving us.We discover the reasons why we do the things we do (or don&rsquo;t do the things that we want to do or should do).This gives us the opportunity to start to consciously reconcile these hidden aspects so that we can achieve our deepest desires or reach our fullest potential.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>Even the Hero, who exudes bravery has a shadow side, and in many cases for The Hero, some of their shadow self is likely to be not quite as courageous as the Hero would like.However, facing this situation is the key to unlocking true courage and resilience.The question is, dear Hero, are you up for the challenge?</p>
                <p><strong>The Hero&rsquo;s Hidden Self</strong></p>
                <p>If people who demonstrate the Hero archetype qualities were able to express their deepest desires, they&rsquo;d be likely to want to show others that they mean well, and that they are not ruthless, unkind or even as arrogant as they may occasionally seem.It&rsquo;s just that when there is a job to be done, they have to do the job.The Hero will always do what it takes to get any job done because that is how it should be (according to a Hero archetype).&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>Kindness, and Glory for Kindness Go Hand-in-Hand </strong></p>
                <p>Heroes would probably want you to know that they don&rsquo;t mean to step on people&rsquo;s toes, far from it. They are just trying to help, and if they get a bit of glory for their efforts, then that&rsquo;s not such a bad thing.</p>
                <p><strong>A Hero Archetype Wouldn&rsquo;t Want You to Know That Sometimes They Don&rsquo;t Know What to Do</strong></p>
                <p>And if the Hero were really as brave as they&rsquo;d like to think they are, they&rsquo;d share with you that sometimes they don&rsquo;t know what to do. Sometimes, they lose sleep at night over trivial things just like everybody else. They want to be loved and respected, and occasionally they&rsquo;d love to be able to move aside and let someone else take over the burden of the responsibilities they place on themselves, but they just can&rsquo;t hand over the reins.&nbsp;</p>
                <p><strong>An Innate Fear of Weakness</strong></p>
                <p>The Hero&rsquo;s hidden side is likely to be rooted in their fear of weakness, which can influence their ego and self-identity.It&rsquo;s this fear of weakness that may inspire most Hero&rsquo;s need to receive the glory for their actions and they quite like it if others can see their efforts too.</p>
                <p>You see, a Hero, might appear to be strong and brave but they don&rsquo;t always know what to do, and this is a problem for a Hero because they see this inability to solve a problem as a weakness.&nbsp;</p>
                <p>The Hero&rsquo;s sense of weakness versus courage is like a double-edged sword; Heroes are courageous but are often afraid to look at or face their biggest weakness. But if they did find the courage to take a look at the weaker side of themselves, they would soon realize that they could conquer it.This means that they would succeed in overcoming their greatest challenge and in doing so, they would become stronger.</p>
                <p><strong>Failing Is Not an Option for A Hero</strong></p>
                <p>If a Hero, fails - it&rsquo;s going to be a tough experience for them.Of course, nobody likes to fail, but the Hero takes failure very personally.</p>
                <p>The hero is someone who has an incredible amount of courage and strength to overcome many of life's problems. Also, he has to overcome these problems because your courageous victories form your identity and sense of self. To top that they also must be somebody who needs the world to know when they are winning.</p>
                <p>The hero is a person whose fear of failure and weakness is so crippling that you are walking on eggshells with every move you make.It&rsquo;s a tough situation and one that the Hero deals with every minute of the day.</p>
                <p>A Hero&rsquo;s innate sense of confidence and esteem is fragile and defined by their heroic tendencies.That&rsquo;s why they need the glory for their actions and why they need to keep on proving that they&rsquo;ve &lsquo;got this.&rsquo;So, if you happen to feel occasionally frustrated by a Hero archetype, help them out by complimenting them, or reminding them that there are others who can help too.It might just give them the break they dream of!</p>
