            <div class="step5-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Strengths</h2>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/mandala-2.png">                
                <p><strong>Resilient</strong></p>
                <p>The hero archetypes friends and loved ones will come to admire and depend on them for their resilience. Their unshaken fight and sacrifice for the good in life will not go unnoticed and will inspire others to ‘do better’.</p>
                <p><strong>Problem Solvers</strong></p>
                <p>People with the Hero archetype personality are not trying to impress when they set out to resolve a problem (although they’ll lap up the well-deserved credit for that too). They are responding to an innate need and ability to fix things.  It’s only after the event that they might realise the rewards for their efforts and then when they realise they’ll want the reward! </p>
                <p><strong>Strong Minded</strong></p>
                <p>A get it done, and get it sorted (or solved) attitude comes naturally to Hero archetypes. They dislike being unable to solve a problem, or expressing their inability to solve a particular problem too which can be misunderstood and frustrating for some. Hero Archetypes will walk to the end of the earth to solve a problem, even if it takes relentless focus and effort to do so. You won’t find a Hero archetype handing over the responsibility for a problem they are involved with too easily. </p>
                <p><strong>Results Oriented</strong></p>
                <p>Hero archetypes combine their focus and diligence toward solving a problem so that it leads them toward accomplishing a resolution.</p>
                <p>They may use unconventional methods such as meditation to help them solve a problem but because they are very black and white they probably don’t realise or admit to themselves that they are using such methods to achieve their goals.  As we have already said, the Hero will do whatever it takes to get the desired result for that’s the only priority for the Hero.</p>
                <p><strong>Dedicated To Their Plight</strong></p>
                <p>When something captures the Hero archetypes attention and speaks to their beliefs, they go all in, dedicating their time, energy, thoughts and emotions to the project.</p>
                <p><strong>Courageous</strong></p>
                <p>While the qualities of others might be more discreet  a hero’s courage will leave nobody in doubt of who is focused on the challenges of the moment. </p>
                
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Weaknesses</h2>
                <p><strong>Afraid Of Being Perceived A Coward</strong></p>
                <p>Hero’s often take their bravado too far, because they are afraid of cowardice and do not want to be perceived as a coward in anyway.  It’s important for those around a Hero archetype to know that they have ‘got this’.</p>
                <p>Unfortunately, as humans we don’t always have everything together, and we are all limited occasionally, even Hero archetypes are limited too.  This can be hard for a Hero to deal with or to accept, for that to a Hero is a weakness.  </p>
                <p><strong>Need To Demonstrate Their Bravery</strong></p>
                <p>Hero archetypes often see themselves as brave, not just because they want to ‘save the world’ but also because they need to be brave, it’s how they identify with themselves. This becomes a problem, as they try to push themselves into other people’s problems, or don’t allow somebody else to prove their capability. For example; in the workplace or a relationship. It can also become a problem if their fight to demonstrate bravery prevents them from stepping aside, taking time out when they need it and in trusting others to manage challenging situations too.  </p>
                <p><strong>Excessive Pride</strong></p>
                <p>The Hero Archetype will find it particularly challenging if they ever lose their sense of pride, so much so that if their pride looks like it is going to be damaged it can send them on a downward spiral faster than you can blink, or cause them rise up in seemingly unnecessary arrogance or aggression.  This can make a Hero archetype become too excessive in their expression of pride and can lead to egotistical, insecure and superficial thinking.</p>
                <p><strong>Acting Without Thinking</strong></p>
                <p>Hero archetypes are often so focused on how they can solve problems that they can often step on other people's toes and also seem a little dramatic (especially when there is nothing much happening).  The Hero’s sensibility toward finding and solving a problem are always on high alert which can make them too protective, or too quick to act and can cause conflict and discomfort in their relationships with others, even though their intentions were pure. They were just trying to help in their own way.</p>
                <p><strong>Taking Over</strong></p>
                <p>Hero archetypes often take challenges personally, rather than as inspiration to work through a problem with others. It’s not that they mean to, it’s just that their radar for a challenge overtakes all other sensibilities and they just want to be the one to save the day. </p>
                <p><strong>Can Be Arrogant</strong></p>
                <p>Hero archetypes are bold, sometimes brash and brave. This makes them notoriously misunderstood, their bravado and fear of weakness cause them to appear arrogant which can make it difficult for the Hero archetype to relate to others.  It will require a patient, observant person to take the time to learn more about the hero’s qualities and understand their weaknesses. </p>
