            <div class="step6-reading">
                <h2 class="step-title"><?php echo $_GET['archetype'];?>'s Greatest Challenge</h2>
                <img class="img-intext" src="/offer/paa/img/success.png">                
                <p>At their best the Hero archetype has no problem in dealing with any type of problem or challenges, they thrive on it which makes them excellent senior managers, consultants, project managers, or emergency response workers.   They are the everyday fixers and as a result of their work, they move the world forward positively. </p>
                <p>In most storylines, the Hero starts to feel a call to adventure, which he often has to reluctantly address.  Of course he’s perfectly capable of handling the adventure even though there are trials and tribulations to be faced.  But the Hero archetype?  Well, they are never reluctant about the call to adventure - not at all.  As soon as there’s an inkling that there is adventure ahead and an opportunity to step up and save the day the Hero archetype is all in.  </p>
                <p>In some cases, they can even create ‘adventure’ so that they can reap the rewards of saving the day and being a Hero. A potentially negative aspect but one that can be managed nonetheless and there’s no one quite like the Hero archetype capable enough to do so. </p>
                <p>A project manager who was working on his own individuation once told us that at work, he would push his planning to the limits and create problems that didn’t need to be there.  He did the same at home with his family.  He discovered that he needed to feel like he had saved the day and in doing so that he would be respected at work and loved at home.</p>
                <p>This is a classic but extreme example of the Hero rising to the call to adventure - and sometimes creating adventure unnecessarily!  As soon as this person realised the error of his ways, in true Hero archetype style it was resolved.</p>
                <p>The Hero’s greatest challenge is ironic in that it requires the Hero to find the courage to look into the depths of their soul and to understand why they are so fragile and dependant upon external rewards such as social glory for a job well done.  The problem is, the Hero is afraid of weakness, but it is this weakness that he or she needs to face just in the same way that they face every other challenge they take on - it’s just that this war is internal and that means it’s personal. </p>
                <p><strong>How A Hero Archetype Can Master Their Greatest Challenge</strong></p>
                <p>Awareness is always the first step to healing or reconciliation. If a Hero can understand and accept their hidden self and the reasons behind why they might do the things they do (even if the Hero is afraid to acknowledge that they have a weakness) then they start to accept themselves for who they are and then really step into the role of a hero, but this time one who has nothing to prove, but everything to gain for their efforts.  </p>
                <p>As a Hero starts to face parts of themselves that they have denied, ran from or have been afraid of, their perspective on the world will change.  An aware Hero is a true hero and one who will reap the social recognition that they desire for their efforts (although now that they have faced their weakness they won’t need social proof of their courage anymore!).</p>
                <p>The Hero's approach to solving problems becomes focused, well intended and admirable which will inspire others into changing the world in a much more profound way this is how the Hero will make a positive mark on the world. </p>
                <p>And all it takes to achieve this innate power is for the Hero to face, accept and acknowledge all of themselves (even the parts that they deem to be undesirable).</p>
